  "notes": "Mid-90s West Side Chicago \"ride music\": a shuffled, triplet-inflected carrier under western and rodeo imagery, written, produced and engineered in-house. ⛔ **The one parameter that separates this from `chicago-drill` is where the backbeat falls, not how fast the record is.** Both sit in the same numeric band — drill 120–145, this 132–146 — and the research is explicit that the difference is the *opposite* backbeat: drill puts the snare on beat 3 with `halfTime: true`, and this is a full-time backbeat on 2 and 4. Everything else follows from that, which is why `halfTime: false` is the first line of the session block and why the invariant test asserts the snare's position rather than the flag. ⛔ **The carrier is the second half of the archetype and it is a *grammar*, not a timbre.** `hihat.base` weights `\"8T\"` over `\"8th\"` so the stream gallops in triplets, and `continuous` is **false** so the triplet grid is never cross-filled with 16ths — a 16th carpet over this would be drill's hat lane wearing a shuffle, which is the exact thing the research says this is not. The *voice* of that carrier is a ride cymbal, and a ride is a kit choice rather than a note choice: `percs.lanes` leads with `ride` so one actually sounds, at a density that reads as a rider rather than a sprinkle. ⚠ **The 808 is deliberately not the bass.** Research: *\"replayed syncopated funk bass with rests, no sustained 808\"*. `bass808.role` is `counter_riff` — not `bassline`, which makes `bass::generate` return an empty lane and would delete the played bass entirely — with `sustain: \"staccato\"`, no distortion and `slideProb` at 0.02, so the 808 is a tuned drum rather than a sub that holds. The played line is `independent_riff` with rests: `mirror_kick` would land it on the kick's own onsets and make one part played twice. ⚠ **Harmony is P-Funk, not soul-jazz**, which is what keeps it out of `boom-bap`'s lane: static `i` or `i–VII` over two bars, dominant sevenths and ninths rather than triads alone, and B-minor and F♯-minor as the poles the research names. `chordDurationBeats` [2, 8] with four `harmonicRhythm` values is what carries chord variety against the distinctness floor — the lane sits on a vamp, so what varies is how long it sits, not what it sits on. ⚠ **INFERRED, and this is why confidence is `medium` rather than `high`.** Sourced: the tempo band and its centre (with the bpmdatabase half-time readings confirming the spine), the 2-and-4 backbeat, the shuffled ride carrier, the funk-guitar harmony source, the replayed bass, the group-chant hook and the in-house production credit. Inferred: every velocity tier, every register, `swing.amount` at 0.62 (the research says shuffled and gives no number — 0.62 is a real gallop short of a full triplet at 0.667), `humanize.quantizeStrength` at 0.82 (played, looser than drill's 0.9 and nowhere near `jerk`), the whole `intervals`/`contour` split, and the mode names. ⚠ **`snare.offGridMs` is 0, and that is a decision rather than a default.** The played feel this lane has is a *human* one and it is carried by `humanize` — `quantizeStrength` 0.82 with real jitter on every lane. `offGridMs` is a **systematic** displacement, the same number on every snare of every bar, and that is `jerk`'s signature rather than this one's; nothing in the research says a Chicago ride record pushes its backbeat late. A 3ms push was authored here on the first pass and the invariant test caught it landing at tick 967 instead of 960. ⚠ **Two things the research asks for that this model cannot say.** (1) The unison group chant of 8 or 16 bars is a *vocal* arrangement and there is no vocal lane; what carries it here is `melody.structure: \"riff_loop\"` with a low `emptyBarBias`, a hook-shaped riff rather than a chant. (2) Double-time syllable density in the verses is likewise vocal, and nothing in the beat should imitate it.",
